Z||000000||ka4nkf||10-22-2000||10:49 AM||||I have heard for many years that If you tighten the lug nuts to tight that it will warp your rotors. how can this be true when the lug nut studs does not touch the rotors. the studs are attached to the hub and does not ttouch the rotors. I just cant see how over tighten the lug nuts could warp the rotors. I woul say that heat Is what warps the rotors||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||10-22-2000||12:22 PM||||There area allot of myths about stuff on cars. Some of it is actually based in fact from a time when the technology of automobiles was pretty weak. Other myths are crossovers from race to street cars or viseversa.<P>I've heard this about lug nuts myself for many years. I've never seen any proof of it on the cast iron rotors used on the vast majority of cars. I've seen the warning about it in many sources. I can't determin for certain if it's a myth held over from something else or what. It would seem to me that you'd have to do significant damage to the wheel to warp the rather heavy rotor and/or hub.<P>On the other hand I can tell you for certain heat will warp rotors. As I understand it the problem isn't actually heat itself but uneven heating/cooling that does the work on the rotors. In fact you need a certain amount of heat in the brakes for most pad compounds to work their best. Some compounds aren't worth a dam unless they are hot. <P>The rotors never heat and cool perfectly even but the worst is when you get the rotors real hot and then park. The brake pads insulate the rotor keeping the area under them hot longer than the rest of the rotor.<P>Heating over design spec will warp them but you'll likely know you've done this because the brakes will fade at the same time. This specific problem is why we have vented rotors now.<P>Another myth is about vented rotors themselves. Allot of people think that vented rotors are always better than solid. This simply isn't true. Even NASCAR uses solid rotors depending on what track they are at. The rear rotors on every car I have seen are solid, even on very expencive BMW Lexus and Mercedes cars. GM's muscle products is all solid rears and these cars are all V8 powered.<P>For the last several months I've been looking thru the wheel at the rear rotors of cars trying to find even one that had vented rotors. I can't find a single car so far. I definatly won't be doing the grand am thing to the back of my car. Maybe if I was racing I'd consider it but not for street. On the front now,,, that's definatly getting the upgrade. One or both my front rotors are warped as hell.||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||10-22-2000||12:35 PM||||I've wondered about that before too. On the older 70's GM cars with big thick rotors, I never worried about torquing them to specs and never had a problem. But, the new cars, especially the Japanese ones have thin rotors now that are much more prone to warpage.<P>When I was married, my wife took her Nisson Sentra in and got new tires. A couple days later she complained to me that the brake pedal was pulsating during stops. Sure enough the rotor was warped on one side of the front and it wasn't like that before the tire change (I had drove the car a day or two before getting new tires). I checked the lugs with a torque wrench and found the tire place had severely overtighted two of the lug nuts on the side with the warped rotor. Coincidence? I think not! I raised holy-hell with the tire place and they repaired the damage, no charge.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||10-22-2000||12:50 PM||||Good point Bats, I to am used to working mostly on "detroit iron". These new econobox crap heaps go cheap on everything. I'm not suprised that happend. Looks like how much lug torque matters depends on the specific car more than anything.<P>I guess the rule would be that if you don't know for sure then grab the torque wrench and be safe. The torque wrench is cheaper than a brake job.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 10-22-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Seanh||10-22-2000||01:05 PM||||I just thought I would mention this, not to start any wars or anything, but, both my parents 90 and 96 Lincoln Towncars have vented rotors all around. I like it, even without ABS on the 90, that thing will stop on a dime.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000005||StuGood||10-22-2000||01:23 PM||||Of course, the <B>worst</B> part about putting wheels on with too much torque?  Eventually, somebody's going to have to take the wheel off again  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">!<P>Don't know why people get so "enthused" about tightening things - are they afraid a wheel is going to fall off?  If I don't have a torque wrench handy, I just snug them down in sequence and then give them a "little" more - hard to describe.  Never had a wheel fall off in my life.<P>I figure, if a wheel actually started to get loose, there're enough threads that you'd get some serious slop before the wheel actually came off.  Enough to give you a clue that you really need to pull over and see what's up.<P>I have heard that some alloy wheels can expand enough from heating that it could actually fail the studs in tension if they were overtorqued when cold.  Kind of makes sense to me.  In any case, use a torque wrench if you've got one.    ||||reg||14
Z||000006||theogre||10-22-2000||02:52 PM||||No war here.... I hadn't seen a town car. I was mostly looking at things the same rough size & weight as Fiero like BMW camaro/firebird small mercedes and one porche. (I couldn't see the porche well. looked solid.)<P>I hadn't looked at towncar just because they are visibly bigger and I just assumed a bunch heavier. Same for caddy. Just for the record all the trucks I've seen with rear disk have vented rotors too. (The trucks use a drum on the drive shaft for the e brake.)||||reg||1
Z||000007||Max The Chainsaw||10-22-2000||11:31 PM||||hehehe  I always heard from the older Mechanics when i first went into this occupation to "Tighten it till it breaks and back off one eighth turn."<P>I wouldn't recommend it though.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
